steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1787655319
      nanos: 591949438
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "# MASTER PROMPT \342\200\224 Paste this entire block into Google Antigravity\n\n---\n\n## ROLE\n\nYou are a senior full-stack + blockchain engineer building a complete, working, demo-ready MVP for a national student hackathon (Smart India Hackathon, PS SIH26193). You are the ONLY engineer on this. There is no second agent, no separate frontend build, no separate backend build handed off to someone else to connect. You own the entire technical stack end-to-end, including every decision about languages, frameworks, database, and how the pieces talk to each other. The person running you will only handle deployment \342\200\224 everything else, including making sure frontend and backend actually work together correctly out of the box, is your job. Do not hand back a frontend and backend that don\'t connect. Test the full connection yourself before declaring completion.\n\n**You have full freedom to choose the technical stack.** Nothing below is a mandate \342\200\224 treat it as one reasonable set of suggestions, not a spec you must follow. If you judge a different language, framework, database, or architecture will produce a stronger, more reliable, more demo-safe result, use your judgment and choose it. Your only real constraints are the functional requirements and hard requirements sections further down \342\200\224 everything else is yours to decide.\n\n---\n\n## PROJECT\n\n**Name:** Blue Carbon Registry \342\200\224 Blockchain-Audited Blue Carbon MRV & Credit Registry for Indian Mangrove Ecosystems\n\n**One-line pitch:** A platform that watches Indian mangrove forests via satellite/drone/sensor data, calculates how much CO2 they\'ve absorbed, and mints tamper-proof digital carbon credits for verified, India-calibrated results \342\200\224 with a built-in safety reserve in case the mangroves are later destroyed by a cyclone or fire.\n\n**What must be demo-able in ~3-4 weeks, on a live screen, in front of judges, with no internet dependency failures:**\n1. A dashboard showing a mangrove site with live/simulated health data\n2. A \"run verification\" action that pulls in satellite + drone + sensor data, checks consensus, and calculates carbon tonnage\n3. Credits minting automatically once consensus passes, visibly appearing in an audit-trail ledger\n4. A visible buffer-reserve split (e.g. 80% tradeable / 20% held back) shown explicitly, not just claimed\n5. A \"retire credit\" action that permanently burns a credit and updates the ledger\n6. The full audit trail (every mint, every retirement) viewable and provably tamper-evident\n\n---\n\n## THE ONE ARCHITECTURAL PRINCIPLE THAT IS NOT OPTIONAL\n\nWhatever stack you choose, the end result must be **ONE application that runs from ONE command**, with the frontend and backend genuinely wired together \342\200\224 not two separately built things the human has to connect by hand afterward. How you achieve that (same-origin static serving, a monorepo with a single dev-server proxy, a single containerized process, whatever pattern fits your chosen stack) is entirely your call. What\'s non-negotiable is the outcome: one command, zero manual wiring, frontend and backend actually talking to each other when it starts up.\n\nThe README must document exactly one command to get the whole thing running locally. If it takes more than one command, fix it before finishing.\n\n---\n\n## SUGGESTED STACK (a reasonable default \342\200\224 override freely if you have a better plan)\n\n- Backend: FastAPI (Python) or an equivalent you prefer\n- Frontend: React + Vite + TailwindCSS, or an equivalent you prefer\n- Data layer: Firebase Firestore, with a local fallback (e.g. JSON file or SQLite) so the app runs with zero external setup \342\200\224 whatever you choose, it should not require the person running it to configure credentials before the demo works\n- AI/analysis layer: Google Gemini API for anomaly detection across the 3 data sources and generating human-readable verification summaries, with a deterministic mock fallback if no API key is present\n- Satellite/drone/IoT data: mock-data-generator by default (`DATA_SOURCE_MODE=mock`), with a real-integration path stubbed in for later (Sentinel Hub/Copernicus or similar) \342\200\224 do not make the demo depend on a live satellite API key arriving in time\n- Blockchain/audit-trail layer: a genuine hash-linked ledger (each entry hashes its own content + the previous entry\'s hash, tamper detectable by recomputing the chain) rather than integrating a real public/testnet blockchain \342\200\224 a live network dependency is a demo-day risk you don\'t need. This satisfies the \"permissioned blockchain audit trail\" pitch, since the project is explicitly positioned as an audit trail, not a trading exchange.\n\nYou may replace any of the above with something you judge better \342\200\224 these exist to save you decision time, not to box you in.\n\n---\n\n## FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS (these ARE non-negotiable, regardless of stack)\n\n**Pipeline the app must implement:**\n1. Pull data from three independent sources (satellite, drone, ground sensors) for a given mangrove site\n2. Run a consensus check \342\200\224 do the sources agree within a reasonable tolerance before anything is minted?\n3. If consensus passes, calculate estimated carbon tonnage sequestered\n4. Mint credits equal to that tonnage, split into a tradeable portion and a buffer-reserve portion held back for reversal risk\n5. Record every mint as a new, hash-linked, tamper-evident ledger entry\n6. Support retiring (permanently burning) a credit, logged as its own ledger entry\n7. Support re-verifying the entire ledger chain on demand, detecting tampering if any record has been altered\n\n**Buffer-reserve mechanism (this is the project\'s key differentiator \342\200\224 make it real, not decorative):**\n- Default 20% of every mint held in reserve (configurable)\n- A reversal trigger: if a site\'s next verification shows significant degradation (e.g. canopy loss) versus its last verified reading, automatically burn a proportional amount of that site\'s held reserve and log it as a distinct \"reversal event\" in the ledger\n- Demonstrate this working with at least one seeded example scenario before declaring the build done\n\n**Required screens/views:**\n1. Dashboard / site list with total credits minted, per-site status\n2. Site detail page: current readings from all 3 sources, a \"Run Verification\" action, and a generated plain-language summary of the result\n3. Ledger / audit trail view showing the hash-chain linkage visually, so \"tamper-proof\" is obvious without you having to explain it verbally\n4. Credits view: table filterable by status (tradeable / held in reserve / retired), with a retire action\n5. A prominent \"Verify Ledger Integrity\" action \342\200\224 this is the live demo moment. It should visibly confirm the chain is intact, and ideally support a dev-only toggle to deliberately corrupt one record so the verification catching it can be demoed live\n\n---\n\n## HARD REQUIREMENTS BEFORE YOU CONSIDER THIS DONE\n\n1. The single start command brings up a fully working app using mock data \342\200\224 missing API keys or credentials must never crash it.\n2. Running \"Run Verification\" on a site actually produces a new ledger entry and updates the UI without a manual refresh.\n3. The ledger verify action actually recomputes the chain rather than always returning true \342\200\224 prove this to yourself by simulating a tampered record and confirming it correctly flags as invalid.\n4. Buffer-reserve and reversal-trigger logic is real, callable, and demonstrated with at least one working seeded scenario.\n5. Nothing on screen is a hardcoded fake number disconnected from the backend \342\200\224 everything visible is real data flowing through the pipeline, even where the underlying satellite data is mocked.\n6. The README explains, to someone who did not build this: setup, how the demo flow works end-to-end, and a short \"what to say to judges\" script tying back to the buffer-reserve differentiator and the contrast with the Toucan Protocol tokenized-carbon-credit failure (2021-22 case where ~28% of tokenized credits turned out to be low-quality/\"zombie\" credits) as the cautionary case this design avoids.\n\n---\n\n## OUT OF SCOPE \342\200\224 DO NOT DO THIS\n\n- Do not deploy anything \342\200\224 deployment is handled separately, by hand, after you finish.\n- Do not integrate a real public blockchain network requiring live network access during the demo.\n- Do not require a real Sentinel-2/Copernicus account to run \342\200\224 mock mode must be fully functional standalone.\n- Do not build user authentication/login flows \342\200\224 not needed for the demo.\n\n---\n\nBuild the entire thing now, end-to-end, choosing whatever stack you judge best, and self-test the frontend-backend connection before declaring completion."
    items {
      text: "# MASTER PROMPT \342\200\224 Paste this entire block into Google Antigravity\n\n---\n\n## ROLE\n\nYou are a senior full-stack + blockchain engineer building a complete, working, demo-ready MVP for a national student hackathon (Smart India Hackathon, PS SIH26193). You are the ONLY engineer on this. There is no second agent, no separate frontend build, no separate backend build handed off to someone else to connect. You own the entire technical stack end-to-end, including every decision about languages, frameworks, database, and how the pieces talk to each other. The person running you will only handle deployment \342\200\224 everything else, including making sure frontend and backend actually work together correctly out of the box, is your job. Do not hand back a frontend and backend that don\'t connect. Test the full connection yourself before declaring completion.\n\n**You have full freedom to choose the technical stack.** Nothing below is a mandate \342\200\224 treat it as one reasonable set of suggestions, not a spec you must follow. If you judge a different language, framework, database, or architecture will produce a stronger, more reliable, more demo-safe result, use your judgment and choose it. Your only real constraints are the functional requirements and hard requirements sections further down \342\200\224 everything else is yours to decide.\n\n---\n\n## PROJECT\n\n**Name:** Blue Carbon Registry \342\200\224 Blockchain-Audited Blue Carbon MRV & Credit Registry for Indian Mangrove Ecosystems\n\n**One-line pitch:** A platform that watches Indian mangrove forests via satellite/drone/sensor data, calculates how much CO2 they\'ve absorbed, and mints tamper-proof digital carbon credits for verified, India-calibrated results \342\200\224 with a built-in safety reserve in case the mangroves are later destroyed by a cyclone or fire.\n\n**What must be demo-able in ~3-4 weeks, on a live screen, in front of judges, with no internet dependency failures:**\n1. A dashboard showing a mangrove site with live/simulated health data\n2. A \"run verification\" action that pulls in satellite + drone + sensor data, checks consensus, and calculates carbon tonnage\n3. Credits minting automatically once consensus passes, visibly appearing in an audit-trail ledger\n4. A visible buffer-reserve split (e.g. 80% tradeable / 20% held back) shown explicitly, not just claimed\n5. A \"retire credit\" action that permanently burns a credit and updates the ledger\n6. The full audit trail (every mint, every retirement) viewable and provably tamper-evident\n\n---\n\n## THE ONE ARCHITECTURAL PRINCIPLE THAT IS NOT OPTIONAL\n\nWhatever stack you choose, the end result must be **ONE application that runs from ONE command**, with the frontend and backend genuinely wired together \342\200\224 not two separately built things the human has to connect by hand afterward. How you achieve that (same-origin static serving, a monorepo with a single dev-server proxy, a single containerized process, whatever pattern fits your chosen stack) is entirely your call. What\'s non-negotiable is the outcome: one command, zero manual wiring, frontend and backend actually talking to each other when it starts up.\n\nThe README must document exactly one command to get the whole thing running locally. If it takes more than one command, fix it before finishing.\n\n---\n\n## SUGGESTED STACK (a reasonable default \342\200\224 override freely if you have a better plan)\n\n- Backend: FastAPI (Python) or an equivalent you prefer\n- Frontend: React + Vite + TailwindCSS, or an equivalent you prefer\n- Data layer: Firebase Firestore, with a local fallback (e.g. JSON file or SQLite) so the app runs with zero external setup \342\200\224 whatever you choose, it should not require the person running it to configure credentials before the demo works\n- AI/analysis layer: Google Gemini API for anomaly detection across the 3 data sources and generating human-readable verification summaries, with a deterministic mock fallback if no API key is present\n- Satellite/drone/IoT data: mock-data-generator by default (`DATA_SOURCE_MODE=mock`), with a real-integration path stubbed in for later (Sentinel Hub/Copernicus or similar) \342\200\224 do not make the demo depend on a live satellite API key arriving in time\n- Blockchain/audit-trail layer: a genuine hash-linked ledger (each entry hashes its own content + the previous entry\'s hash, tamper detectable by recomputing the chain) rather than integrating a real public/testnet blockchain \342\200\224 a live network dependency is a demo-day risk you don\'t need. This satisfies the \"permissioned blockchain audit trail\" pitch, since the project is explicitly positioned as an audit trail, not a trading exchange.\n\nYou may replace any of the above with something you judge better \342\200\224 these exist to save you decision time, not to box you in.\n\n---\n\n## FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS (these ARE non-negotiable, regardless of stack)\n\n**Pipeline the app must implement:**\n1. Pull data from three independent sources (satellite, drone, ground sensors) for a given mangrove site\n2. Run a consensus check \342\200\224 do the sources agree within a reasonable tolerance before anything is minted?\n3. If consensus passes, calculate estimated carbon tonnage sequestered\n4. Mint credits equal to that tonnage, split into a tradeable portion and a buffer-reserve portion held back for reversal risk\n5. Record every mint as a new, hash-linked, tamper-evident ledger entry\n6. Support retiring (permanently burning) a credit, logged as its own ledger entry\n7. Support re-verifying the entire ledger chain on demand, detecting tampering if any record has been altered\n\n**Buffer-reserve mechanism (this is the project\'s key differentiator \342\200\224 make it real, not decorative):**\n- Default 20% of every mint held in reserve (configurable)\n- A reversal trigger: if a site\'s next verification shows significant degradation (e.